\name{LatticeHeatmapLayerProcessor}
\alias{LatticeHeatmapLayerProcessor}
\title{lattice Heatmap Layer Processor}
\description{
Reads the cells a \code{levelplot()} panel fills as a \code{heat} layer: a grid of
values, with the levels or positions of \code{x} along its columns and of \code{y}
down its rows. A position the data has no row for, or a row whose value
is missing or lies outside \code{at}, which lattice leaves unfilled, is a hole
(\code{null}). A numeric position is named by its value
on the data's own scale, as the axis labels it, rather than by the
logarithm lattice hands a log-scale panel; a date or date-time by the
instant it stands for, which the axis' time format reads out.

\strong{Selectors.} The cells are drawn in the order of the panel's rows --
for a matrix, row by row from the bottom -- which is neither order the
frontend reads one selector for a whole heat grid in. So each cell is
named by its own id, in a grid whose rows run bottom first, as the
frontend's heat grid does, with \code{null} for a cell that was not drawn.
}
\keyword{internal}
